<<<
See also NUMBER. 5. Is, or has, nothing to do with. Sir,—Why
===
See also ^^NUMBER^^. 5. *Is, or has, nothing to do with. Sir,—Why
>>>

<<<
the following sentence: * The trouble is nothing to do with education’ (Italics mine)?
===
the following sentence:* 'The trouble *is* nothing to do with education' (*Italics mine*)?
>>>

<<<
The facts are, first, that has nothing to do with requires no defence
===
The facts are, first, that *has nothing to do with* requires no defence
>>>

<<<
secondly, that is nothing to do with is said by many to be indefensible
===
secondly, that *is nothing to do with* is said by many to be indefensible
>>>

<<<
& thirdly, that is nothing to do with is nevertheless, if not far commoner
===
& thirdly, that *is nothing to do with* is nevertheless, if not far commoner
>>>

<<<
an attempt to justify 7s.
===
an attempt to justify *is*.
>>>

<<<
feel that That is nothing to do with you expresses our feelings better than That has &c.
===
feel that *That is nothing to do with you* expresses our feelings better than *That has* &c.
>>>

<<<
the instinctive word is is, not has.
===
the instinctive word is *is*, not *has*.
>>>

<<<
the thing: That is nothing to you, & That has nothing to do with you.
===
the thing: *That is nothing to you*, & *That has nothing to do with you*.
>>>

<<<
Now it does not seem impossible that It is nothing to do with may have arisen
===
Now it does not seem impossible that *It is nothing to do with* may have arisen
>>>

<<<
in which to do has acquired the status of an adjective meaning concerned or connected; such sentences would
===
in which *to do* has acquired the status of an adjective meaning *concerned* or *connected*; such sentences would
>>>

<<<
+ be: There is nothing to do with prisons that he cannoi tell you. A Wife’s Secret’ (nothing to do with the old play of that name)./ Anything io do with spiritualism is interesting.
===
+ be: *There is nothing to do with prisons that he cannot tell you.*/*'A Wife's Secret' (nothing to do with the old play of that name).*/*Anything to do with spiritualism is interesting.*
>>>

<<<
In the first of those nothing to do means not a single thing concerned, nothing being a noun
===
In the first of those *nothing to do* means not a single thing concerned, *nothing* being a noun
>>>

<<<
& in the second it means not at all connected, nothing being an adverb.
===
& in the second it means not at all connected, *nothing* being an adverb.
>>>

<<<
No doubt this use of io do is elliptical for having to do; but the point is
===
No doubt this use of *to do* is elliptical for *having to do*; but the point is
>>>

<<<
gives us a different construction for nothing or any corresponding word
===
gives us a different construction for *nothing* or any corresponding word
>>>

<<<
not the object of the omitted having, as it is of has in Jt has nothing to do with, but is either the noun with which the supposed having agrees or an adverb negativing it.
===
not the object of the omitted *having*, as it is of *has* in *It has nothing to do with*, but is either the noun with which the supposed *having* agrees or an adverb negativing it.
>>>

<<<
the two forms may be paraphrased thus: Ii has nothing to do with you = It has no function to perform with you; & Ii is nothing to do with you=It is not a matter concerned with you;
===
the two forms may be paraphrased thus: *It has nothing to do with you* = It has *no function* to perform with you; & *It is nothing to do with you*=It is *not a matter* concerned with you;
>>>

<<<
not only in the visible distinction between has & is, but also in the invisible one between two or more constructions of nothing.
===
not only in the visible distinction between *has* & *is*, but also in the invisible one between two or more constructions of *nothing*.
>>>

<<<
there are three right ways of saying the thing: It is nothing to you; It has nothing to do with you; It is nothing to do with you: instead of two right ways & a wrong.
===
there are three right ways of saying the thing: *It is nothing to you*; *It has nothing to do with you*; *It is nothing to do with you*: instead of two right ways & a wrong.
>>>

